{"purpose":"Establish what is actually implementable for audio capture and push-to-talk inside a terminal, across the target device mix, before the voice requirement and the runtime stack decision are fixed.","content":"## Why the latency figure cannot yet be treated as met\n\nThe budget above is **calculated, not measured**. Every term in it is a nominal value; none has been observed on the target devices. In particular the jitter buffer figure assumes a well-behaved network, and the ARM devices in the fleet have not been profiled for encode cost at all.\n\nThe honest position: the sub-100 ms target is credible on direct paths and not credible on relayed paths, and a spike must produce measured numbers on real hardware before any requirement is treated as satisfied.\n\n## Codec parameters this points to\n\nFor speech in a terminal tool, wideband mono at 16 kHz with 20 ms frames and roughly 16–24 kbps is the sensible operating point. Two Opus features matter disproportionately here:\n\n- **DTX** (discontinuous transmission) collapses bandwidth toward zero during silence, which is most of a conversation. This matters directly for the bandwidth-constrained locations in the vision.\n- **PLC and in-band FEC** absorb isolated packet loss without retransmission, which is the only workable approach when a retransmitted frame would arrive after its playback deadline.\n\n## Framing\n\nRTP framing and jitter buffering should be reused rather than invented — sequence numbers, timestamps and loss signalling are a solved problem with mature Go implementations. Inventing a bespoke audio wire format would be re-solving it worse.","investigationSummary":"Examined four areas. (1) Audio capture options available to a Go program without GUI dependencies: miniaudio through the malgo binding, which covers ALSA and PulseAudio on Linux, CoreAudio on macOS and WASAPI on Windows; and PortAudio as the heavier alternative. (2) Opus codec availability in Go: encoding requires libopus through CGO, while the available pure-Go implementation covers decoding only. (3) Terminal input capability for key-release events: standard terminal input reports key presses but no key-up event; the Kitty keyboard protocol's event-type reporting does report releases, and is implemented by kitty, foot, WezTerm and Ghostty. (4) CGO cross-compilation to ARM targets, and the cost of maintaining per-target C sysroots versus using zig cc as the cross compiler.","conclusion":"**Three constraints bind the design.**\n\n1. **Any audio path requires CGO.** There is no viable pure-Go Opus encoder, and capture itself is a C library binding. Audio must therefore sit behind a build tag, so that the default build remains a pure-Go static binary serving text, presence and files, and the audio-capable build is a deliberate superset. This preserves the 'degrade rather than fail' principle at build level.\n\n2. **Hold-to-talk is not portably implementable.** A terminal does not deliver key-release events, so 'hold the key while speaking' cannot be built portably. The portable interaction is a **toggle**: tap to start capture, tap to send. Hold-to-talk is available only as an opt-in enhancement on terminals where the Kitty keyboard protocol is detected. This is a constraint discovered by investigation, not a product preference.\n\n3. **CGO cross-compilation to linux/arm64 and linux/arm is tractable using zig cc** as the C compiler, which avoids maintaining a sysroot per target.\n\n**Latency budget for real-time voice** (stage 2, not the MVP): 20 ms capture frame + roughly 1 ms encode + network + 40–60 ms adaptive jitter buffer + 20 ms playback. That lands near 85–105 ms mouth-to-ear on a direct, nearby path, and materially higher over a DERP relay, where an extra round trip to the relay is added."}